     "title": "Unearned revenue: customer deposits are a liability",
     "text": "Definition. Unearned revenue is money received from a customer for goods or services the business has not yet delivered. It is a liability. The business owes the customer the delivery, or a refund. As the business delivers, the liability shrinks and revenue is recognized.\n\nWhy a liability. The cash has come in, but the work has not been done. The business has taken on an obligation. If the business closed tomorrow, it would owe that money back. Counting it as revenue now would overstate the period's results and hide the duty to deliver.\n\nThe mirror image of a receivable. In S5, the work is done and the cash is not yet in. Here, the cash is in and the work is not yet done. Accrual accounting follows the work in both cases. Revenue follows delivery, not payment, in either direction.\n\nHow it unwinds. For a prepaid service that runs evenly over time, the revenue is recognized evenly. Each month of the service moves one month's share from unearned revenue to revenue.\n\nWorked example. A studio sells a 12-month plan for 3,600, paid in full on 1 November. The plan starts that day.\n1. Monthly share = 3,600 ÷ 12 = 300.\n2. By 31 December, two months have been delivered (November and December).\n3. Revenue earned = 2 × 300 = 600.\n4. Unearned revenue at 31 December = 3,600 − 600 = 3,000.\n5. The 3,000 stays on the balance sheet as a liability and moves to revenue at 300 a month through the following ten months.\n\nOn 1 November the studio had 3,600 more cash and a liability of 3,600. Total equity did not move on that day.\n\nCommon mistake. Recording the whole 3,600 as revenue on the day of the payment. Cash is not the test. Delivery is. The full amount would sit in the first period, and the next ten months would look as if the studio earned nothing."
