     "title": "Liquidity: working capital and the current ratio",
     "text": "Definition. Liquidity is the ability to pay bills as they fall due. Current assets are cash and the assets expected to turn into cash within a year, such as receivables and inventory. Current liabilities are the debts due within a year. Working capital = current assets − current liabilities. The current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities.\n\nWhat each measures. Working capital is a dollar cushion. It says how much would be left over if every current asset were turned into cash and every current debt were paid. The current ratio is the same comparison as a multiple. A ratio of 1.0 means the business has exactly one dollar of current assets for each dollar of current liabilities. A ratio above 1.0 means a cushion. A ratio below 1.0 means that the short-term debts are larger than the short-term assets.\n\nReading the ratio carefully. The ratio treats all current assets alike. But cash pays a bill at once, while inventory must first be sold and a receivable must first be collected. Two businesses with the same ratio can have very different ability to pay this week. That is why analysts also look at how much of the current assets is cash.\n\nWorked example. A garden shop holds Cash 4,000, Accounts receivable 6,000 and Inventory 5,000. Its current liabilities are 12,000.\n1. Current assets = 4,000 + 6,000 + 5,000 = 15,000.\n2. Working capital = 15,000 − 12,000 = 3,000.\n3. Current ratio = 15,000 ÷ 12,000 = 1.25.\n4. Cash alone covers 4,000 ÷ 12,000 = one third of the current liabilities.\n\nThe shop looks safe on the ratio. Yet most of its cushion is stock still to be sold and bills still to be collected. If suppliers demanded payment this week, only the 4,000 of cash would be available at once.\n\nCommon mistake. Treating a ratio over 1.0 as a guarantee. The ratio says nothing about timing. A business can have a good ratio and still miss a payroll, because the inventory has not sold yet."
