    {
     "id": "S9",
     "title": "The three statements, and why profit is not cash",
     "text": "Definition. Financial statements report on a business in three linked ways. The income statement reports revenue, expenses and net income over a period. The balance sheet reports assets, liabilities and owner's equity at one date. The cash flow statement reports where cash came from and where it went over a period. Net income links the first to the second: it is added to owner's equity. Cash links the second to the third: the ending cash on the cash flow statement is the cash on the balance sheet.\n\nHow they link. Each period, owner's equity at the end = equity at the start + net income − what the owner withdrew. The cash flow statement then explains the change in the cash line. Together, the three give a full picture. If one is changed, the others must follow, or the balance sheet will stop balancing.\n\nProfit is not cash. Net income is measured on the accrual basis. Cash is measured as it comes and goes. The two differ whenever there are receivables, inventory purchases, payables or prepayments. A business can earn a profit and see its cash rise by less, or even fall.\n\nWorked example. A small shop starts the year with Cash 5,000 and owner's equity 5,000. Nothing else exists. During the year it sells goods and buys stock on credit. The owner withdraws nothing.\n\nIncome statement for the year:\n- Revenue 60,000\n- Cost of goods sold 36,000\n- Gross profit 24,000\n- Wages 10,000\n- Rent 4,000\n- Net income 10,000\n\nBalance sheet at year end:\n- Cash 8,000\n- Accounts receivable 6,000\n- Inventory 4,000\n- Total assets 18,000\n- Accounts payable 3,000\n- Owner's equity 15,000 (5,000 + 10,000)\n- Total liabilities and equity 18,000\n\nCash flow statement for the year:\n- Net income 10,000\n- Increase in receivables −6,000\n- Increase in inventory −4,000\n- Increase in payables +3,000\n- Cash from operations 3,000\n- Cash at start 5,000, cash at end 8,000\n\nCheck the links. Assets of 18,000 equal liabilities of 3,000 plus equity of 15,000. Cash rose from 5,000 to 8,000, which is the 3,000 from operations. The shop earned 10,000 and gained only 3,000 of cash. The other 7,000 sits in customers' unpaid bills and in stock, net of what is owed to suppliers.\n\nCommon mistake. Reading net income as the change in the bank balance. The profit is real, but the bank balance moved by less, and the difference is explained by the cash flow statement."
    },
    {
     "id": "S10",
     "title": "The cash flow statement, indirect method",
     "text": "Definition. The indirect method is a way of finding cash from operations. It starts with net income and adjusts it for changes in the balance sheet accounts that tie to operations. These adjustments undo the gap between accrual profit and cash.\n\nThe rule for each adjustment. Ask what a change in the balance sheet line did to cash.\n- An increase in a receivable means sales were recorded but not yet collected. Cash is lower than profit. Subtract the increase.\n- A decrease in a receivable means customers paid old bills. Cash is higher than profit. Add the decrease.\n- An increase in inventory means cash was spent on stock that is still unsold. Subtract the increase.\n- A decrease in inventory means stock sold that was paid for earlier. Add the decrease.\n- An increase in payables means expenses were recorded but not yet paid. Cash is higher than profit. Add the increase.\n- A decrease in payables means old bills were paid. Subtract the decrease.\n\nThe pattern is easy to remember. A rise in an asset uses cash, and a rise in a liability provides cash.\n\nWorked example. Net income for the year is 18,000. Accounts receivable rose by 2,500, inventory fell by 1,200 and accounts payable fell by 700.\n1. Start with net income = 18,000.\n2. Receivables rose: subtract 2,500, which leaves 15,500.\n3. Inventory fell: add 1,200, which gives 16,700.\n4. Payables fell: subtract 700, which gives 16,000.\n5. Cash from operations = 16,000.\n\nApply the same method to the shop in S9. Net income of 10,000, less 6,000 for the rise in receivables, less 4,000 for the rise in inventory, plus 3,000 for the rise in payables gives 3,000. That matches the cash flow statement there, and it matches the actual change in the bank balance.\n\nCommon mistake. Getting the signs backwards. A reader sees \"inventory rose\" and adds the increase because the number is positive. The sign follows the effect on cash. A rise in an asset takes cash out, so it is subtracted."
    }
